# Code Conventions

- Prefer east-const style where applicable (for example, `T const` over `const T`).
- Prefer `const` for variables and parameters that are not reassigned when it
  improves clarity; apply east-const style (`T const value`).
- Keep declarations stylistically consistent in code you are actively editing.
- Do not mass-reformat untouched files only to enforce style; apply conventions incrementally as files are changed for functional work.
- For long function signatures, prefer one-parameter-per-line formatting (like `ComptonKernelQuadrature::sigma_E`) over compact multi-parameter single-line forms.

```cpp
// ❌ BAD
const T gamma_t = static_cast<T>(gamma);

// ✅ GOOD
T const gamma_t = static_cast<T>(gamma);
```
